From eaf42e6bec9cdd7ed98afe2e35938252b8574bcd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noah Evans <noah.evans@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 06:40:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] hw/arm/bcm2838: give the ARM timer a line to interrupt on

bcm_soc_peripherals_common_realize() connects the ARM timer to the
legacy interrupt controller's ARM bank, for every BCM SoC including
this one, and nothing connects that controller to the GIC on a Pi 4.
So on raspi4b the timer's interrupt was delivered to a controller
nothing dispatches: the block is mapped, it counts, it raises its
interrupt, and no CPU ever sees it.

The two published device trees say the legacy controller is not
cascaded on this part at all -- neither mainline's bcm2711.dtsi nor the
Raspberry Pi tree's has an &intc override, an armctrl node with an
interrupt parent, or any cascade; the root interrupt-parent is the GIC
and peripherals name their own SPIs.  So the fix is to name this one
too.

Its SPI is derived rather than read, and it is the only one in this
file that is.  The VideoCore sources are the legacy numbers plus 64,
which eight pairs confirm: raspi_platform.h has the system timer at 0,
USB at 9, DMA0 at 16, AUX at 29, SDHOST at 56, UART0 at 57, the RNG at
61 and the Arasan at 62, and bcm2711.dtsi gives them GIC_SPI 64, 73,
80, 93, 120, 121, 125 and 126.  The ARM-side sources are the SPIs below
64, and the only one a device tree names is the mailbox at GIC_SPI 33.
In the ARM private numbering, also in raspi_platform.h and eight
interrupts wide, the mailbox is 1 and the ARM timer is 0 -- so the
group is based at 32 and the timer is SPI 32.  The MPHI at SPI 40
agrees, being exactly where a group of eight starting at 32 ends.  No
device tree names the timer because Linux does not use it on a BCM2711.

Leaving it unconnected was not the cautious option: the part has the
line, and delivering the interrupt to a controller nothing dispatches
is a claim about the hardware too, and a worse one.  What is derived is
the number and only the number, and it is written down here and in the
guest driver that depends on it.

Verified with an Inferno kernel on raspi4b, which programs the timer
for 100Hz and counts interrupts against the system timer over 200ms:
19 interrupts in 200024 us, against twenty predicted.  That exercises
the guest's whole GIC path -- priority, target, enable, IAR, handler,
EOI -- on an SPI nothing else in its suite uses.  The board's marker
set gains ARMTIMER-INTR-OK and is otherwise unchanged at 36.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 hw/arm/bcm2838.c                     |  4 ++++
 include/hw/arm/bcm2838_peripherals.h | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/arm/bcm2838.c b/hw/arm/bcm2838.c
index 22aa754..5db6115 100644
--- a/hw/arm/bcm2838.c
+++ b/hw/arm/bcm2838.c
@@ -184,6 +184,10 @@ static void bcm2838_realize(DeviceState *dev, Error **errp)
     sysbus_connect_irq(SYS_BUS_DEVICE(&ps_base->aux), 0,
                        qdev_get_gpio_in(gicdev, GIC_SPI_INTERRUPT_AUX_UART1));
 
+    /* Connect the ARM timer to the interrupt controller */
+    sysbus_connect_irq(SYS_BUS_DEVICE(&ps_base->armtimer), 0,
+                       qdev_get_gpio_in(gicdev, GIC_SPI_INTERRUPT_ARM_TIMER));
+
     /* Connect VC mailbox to the interrupt controller */
     sysbus_connect_irq(SYS_BUS_DEVICE(&ps_base->mboxes), 0,
                        qdev_get_gpio_in(gicdev, GIC_SPI_INTERRUPT_MBOX));
diff --git a/include/hw/arm/bcm2838_peripherals.h b/include/hw/arm/bcm2838_peripherals.h
index 0efa197..3a601a6 100644
--- a/include/hw/arm/bcm2838_peripherals.h
+++ b/include/hw/arm/bcm2838_peripherals.h
@@ -16,6 +16,22 @@
 #include "hw/misc/bcm2838_thermal.h"
 
 /* SPI */
+/*
+ * The ARM-side interrupts occupy the SPIs below 64 and the VideoCore
+ * ones start at 64, one for one with the legacy numbering in
+ * raspi_platform.h: system timer 0 is legacy 0 and GIC_SPI 64,
+ * UART0 is legacy 57 and 121, the DWC2 is 9 and 73, the Arasan is 62
+ * and 126.  bcm2711.dtsi states the SPI side of every one of those.
+ *
+ * The ARM timer's line is the exception and is derived rather than
+ * read: bcm2711.dtsi gives the mailbox GIC_SPI 33, the ARM private
+ * number space in raspi_platform.h has the mailbox at 1 and the ARM
+ * timer at 0, so the group is based at 32 and the timer is 32.  No
+ * device tree names it, because Linux does not use this timer on a
+ * BCM2711.  Leaving it unconnected is certainly wrong -- the part has
+ * the line -- so it is wired here, with the derivation written down.
+ */
+#define GIC_SPI_INTERRUPT_ARM_TIMER    32
 #define GIC_SPI_INTERRUPT_MBOX         33
 #define GIC_SPI_INTERRUPT_MPHI         40
 #define GIC_SPI_INTERRUPT_DWC2         73
-- 
2.51.0

